  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 800,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On paper, $250,000 puts you in the top slice of \nAmerican earners. In reality, a family that layers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9200,
    "dDurationMs": 8640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on a $4,500 mortgage in a prestige zip code, \ntwo private school tuitions at $20,000 each, two  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17840,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "$900 luxury SUVs, and high child care costs can \nburn through more than $140,000 a year in fixed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26240,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expenses before groceries, travel, or retirement \ncontributions. After taxes, that leaves barely  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34320,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "$2,000 a month for everything else, including \nsavings. That is how high earners end up living  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41360,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "paycheck to paycheck and staring down retirement \nwith low six figures instead of the millions they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47440,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would need to sustain their lifestyle. The poverty \nline for a $250,000 household is not about income.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55840,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It is about how much of it you have already \ngiven away to your zip code, your school choice,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60880,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and your car payment. You wake up, you go to \na high stress job. You do everything right  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "according to society, but your bank account at the \nend of the month looks like you work part-time at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72560,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a hardware store. That is the functional \npoverty line, and millions are trapped in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78320,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it. If you want the best return on investment \nfor your time today, hit the subscribe button.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85600,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I am Tom and I spend hours digging through Federal \nReserve data, Yahoo Finance reports, and forum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91760,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "posts so you do not have to. I run the numbers on \nwhat it actually costs to live the American dream.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98400,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today, right now, that dream is getting highly \nexpensive and the math is completely disjointed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105200,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from reality. We are going to walk through \nthe exact breakdown of where a4 million goes.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112000,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We will look at the taxes, the phantom costs, and \nthe specific choices that drain the wealth of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117520,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "top tier. If you are earning a high income but \nfeeling completely broke, you are not crazy. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124880,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "game is rigged against the default path. By the \nend of this conversation, you will have a clear  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130480,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "framework for identifying these fixed cost traps \nand stepping off the treadmill before it grinds  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135760,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your retirement plans to absolute dust. Let us \nstart by looking at the core myth of the high  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141600,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "earner. There is a very common belief that if you \ncan just crack the quarter million mark, all your  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149600,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "financial anxiety will magically evaporate. You \nassume you will be taking European vacations,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156400,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "maxing out every retirement account, and drinking \ncoffee that was ethically sourced by monks. But I  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163840,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have noticed that the higher the income goes, the \nlouder the quiet desperation becomes. This is the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170800,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "setup for what we call the functional poverty \nline. It does not mean literal federal poverty.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177280,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It means a mathematical state where your high \nsalary produces zero surplus. Your income acts  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184240,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a massive river, but your lifestyle choices \nhave built a dam that diverts every single drop  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190320,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before it reaches your savings. The assumption is \nthat wealth equals income. But wealth is actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197840,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what you do not spend. When you cross the $250,000 \nthreshold, society expects you to act like you are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204720,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rich. And acting rich is the fastest way to \nensure you never actually become rich. Let us  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212160,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "run the hard numbers on this because the data \nis staggering. According to CNBC, about 14% of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220080,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "United States households earn $200,000 or more \nannually. Yet, a massive chunk of these people  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228480,
    "dDurationMs": 8800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fall into a category called Henry's. That stands \nfor high earnner, not rich yet. They live paycheck  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237280,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to paycheck and struggle with rolling credit card \ndebt. A recent report summarized by Yahoo Finance  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245040,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "noted that four in 10 workers earning more than \n$500,000 a year say they are making only minimal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252320,
    "dDurationMs": 9120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "progress on their financial goals. Four out of \n10, half a million a year. The Federal Reserve  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261440,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "economic well-being report backs this up, showing \nthat inflation and prices remain the top financial  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268080,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "concern across all income brackets. When I read \nthat, I had to laugh because it proves that out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 275520,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "earning your own spending habits is mathematically \nimpossible. If a $500,000 household is broke,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283600,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a $250,000 household is swimming in dangerous \nwaters. Why do we fail even when we know  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292000,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the math? It comes down to the psychology of \ninvisible spending and lifestyle inflation. When  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299200,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you get that big promotion, your reference group \nimmediately changes. You move to a slightly nicer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304800,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neighborhood where the baseline expectations are \ntotally different. Suddenly, driving a 7-year-old  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310320,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sedan feels like a social failure because your \nneighbors are leasing luxury SUVs. You are not  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316320,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trying to be flashy. You are just trying to keep \nup with the new normal. But that new normal is a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321920,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "financial sinkhole. We convince ourselves that we \ndeserve these upgrades because we work so hard.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328480,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It is the classic trap of tying your self-worth to \nyour fixed monthly overhead. You start justifying  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334880,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every expense as an investment in your family or a \nnecessary convenience for your stressful life. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340880,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your stress is actually coming from the fact that \nyou have locked up 90% of your future labor to pay  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346000,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for things you bought yesterday. The mental toll \nof this invisible trap is absolutely exhausting.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353520,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The solution here requires a complete rewiring \nof how you view your paycheck. The protocol is to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360880,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stop measuring your success by your gross income \nand start measuring it by your free cash flow.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368000,
    "dDurationMs": 8480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Free cash flow is what remains after taxes and \nmandatory fixed costs. If you make $250,000,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376480,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but your fixed lifestyle consumes $230,000 of \nit, you are not a4 million household. You are a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384640,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "$20,000 household. That is your actual functional \nincome. Once you adopt this methodology,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392240,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you stop looking at a bonus as permission to buy \na new car. Instead, you view it as raw material  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398720,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to increase your free cash flow margin. \nThe specific action step is to calculate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404720,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your personal functional income today. Take your \ntake-home pay, subtract your absolute survival,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411840,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and contractual obligations and look at the \ntiny number left over. That is the only number  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418240,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that matters. We need to fiercely protect that \nnumber from the traps we are about to discuss.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425920,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you think the psychological pressure \nof keeping up with the neighbors is bad,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430400,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wait until you see what happens when those \nneighbors start talking about where their kids go  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434720,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to kindergarten. Most people assume the mortgage \nis the biggest wealth destroyer for a high earner.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441920,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there is a silent, entirely optional expense \nthat routinely wipes out 50 to $80,000 of after  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450160,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tax income every single year. And the wildest \npart is that parents will fiercely defend this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457440,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expense while simultaneously complaining that \nthey can never afford to retire. Let us look  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464480,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the absolute black hole of private school \ntuition. The initial situation usually starts  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471440,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with the best of intentions. You have kids and \nyou want to give them every possible advantage.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 477760,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You tour a local private school with pristine \ntennis courts, a robotics lab, and organic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 483040,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lunches. It feels like an entirely different \nworld compared to the crowded public schools  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488560,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you might have grown up in. The common belief is \nthat private education is the ultimate investment  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494960,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in your child's future and that if you have a high \nincome, it is your duty to pay for it. But this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502160,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is where the $250,000 poverty line really begins \nto bear its teeth. What starts as a noble desire  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510080,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for smaller class sizes quickly morphs into an \nabsolute financial chokeold. I have seen families  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517760,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "willingly put themselves on the brink of insolveny \njust to keep a bumper sticker on their car. They  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524320,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "view private school as a non-negotiable utility \nbill right up there with electricity and water.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531120,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But unlike water, the price of this utility goes \nup relentlessly every single year. Let us run the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538720,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hard numbers on private school because they are \nterrifying. According to private school review,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545840,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the national average is around $15,000 a year. But \nif you live in a high-cost metro area, that number  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553440,
    "dDurationMs": 10880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is a joke. Financial Samurai highlighted a Bay \nArea school charging $39,600 per child. Let us be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564320,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "conservative and say you find a moderate private \nschool for $20,000 a year per child. For two kids,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571680,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that is $40,000 annually. But remember, you \npay tuition with after tax dollars. If your  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579440,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "effective tax rate is around 30%, you need to earn \nnearly $57,000 in gross income just to cover the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587200,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tuition check. In places like Washington DC, forum \nposters routinely site spending $86,000 a year  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595360,
    "dDurationMs": 10960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "after tax on two teenagers. That requires over \n$120,000 of gross income. At a $250,000 salary,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606320,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you are spending almost half of your total earning \npower just to keep your kids in plaid uniforms.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612880,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That leaves almost nothing for your own future. \nThe hidden cost here goes far beyond the tuition  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619840,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "check. The psychology of private school is rooted \nin deep parental guilt and social conformity.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 627600,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Why do we fail to make the rational choice here \neven when we know the math is bankrupting us?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634720,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because pulling your kids out of private school \nfeels like a public admission of financial defeat.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641200,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You worry that your kids will fall behind or \nthat you are a bad parent for prioritizing your  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646480,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "retirement over their seventh grade Latin class. \nFurthermore, private schools come with secondary  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653040,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "costs. There are the mandatory fundraisers, \nthe expensive ski trips, the pressure to host  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659200,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lavish birthday parties. You are buying into \na social ecosystem where the median household  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665360,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "income might be double or triple yours. Trying to \nswim in that pool when you are at the poverty line  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672080,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of $250,000 is exhausting. You are essentially \npaying a premium to feel inadequate every time  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680160,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you attend a parent teacher association meeting. \nThe specific action step here is not necessarily  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686800,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to ban all private schooling, but to rigorously \napply a return on investment methodology to it.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693600,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The protocol requires you to calculate what that \n$40,000 a year would do if invested in a simple  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700000,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "index fund. Over 12 years of schooling, that is \nnearly half a million of principal alone before  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707120,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "compounding. The mindset shift is realizing that \nfinancial security is actually a better gift to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713600,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your children than a private high school diploma. \nYou do not want to be a burden on them when you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719280,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are 80 years old because you spent your retirement \nfunds on their middle school tuition. Evaluate the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725840,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "public schools in your area without the bias of \nprestige. Often you will find that an engaged  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731760,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "parent with a stable, stress-free financial life \nproduces a much better outcome for a child than  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738000,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a stressed out parent writing massive tuition \nchecks. Do not let parental guilt mask what is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744240,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
