    "tStartMs": 1526480,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not the absolute size of your paycheck. \nFidelity and other financial institutions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1532000,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "generally suggest saving at least 15% of \nyour gross income if you start in your 20s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1537200,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and want to maintain your current lifestyle. \nBut as we just saw in our master equation,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1542880,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a family locked into heavy fixed costs barely \nhas enough liquid cash to cover groceries,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1548160,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "let alone squirrel away 15%. The trap is that \nhigh earners build a lifestyle that requires  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1554480,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "$20,000 a month to maintain, but they are \nfunding their retirement accounts with the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1559200,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spare change they find in the couch cushions. \nThe math simply does not support the dream.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1566560,
    "dDurationMs": 8880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let us run the hard evidence on this retirement \ngap. On a $250,000 income, a 15% savings rate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1575440,
    "dDurationMs": 9680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "requires putting away $37,500 every single year. \nBut in our fixed cost scenario, the family only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1585120,
    "dDurationMs": 9680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "had $27,000 left for everything. That means their \nrealistic savings rate might be 0 to 5% of gross.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1595760,
    "dDurationMs": 10160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let us assume they managed to save 5%. Which is \n$12,500 a year. Over 30 years, even assuming a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1605920,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solid 7% market return, that only builds a \nnest egg in the low to mid6 figures, maybe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1612960,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around 1.2 million. That sounds like a lot until \nyou realize that replacing a4 million lifestyle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1620880,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "requires $3 to5 million in assets under a safe 4% \nwithdrawal framework. You are mathematically short  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1629280,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by millions. I have read forum posts where parents \nexplicitly admit they are sacrificing their entire  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1636480,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "retirement to pay for elite private schools and \nprestige mortgages. Today they are trading their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1642960,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "financial independence at age 60 for a slightly \nnicer driveway at age 40. The hidden psychology  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1650640,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "here is the delusion of tomorrow. We constantly \nlie to ourselves and say we will start saving  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1657120,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "aggressively once the kids are out of daycare \nor once the car is paid off or once we get that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1663520,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "next promotion. But Parkinson's law of money \ndictates that expenses will always rise to meet  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1669040,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "income unless there is intentional friction \nto stop it. When the daycare bill goes away,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1674480,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it magically turns into club sports fees \nor summer camps. When the car is paid off,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1679520,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the kitchen suddenly needs a remodel. We fail \nto realize that time in the market is the one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1684720,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "variable we can never buy back. Every year you \ndelay compounding because your money is locked  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1689840,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up in a luxury lease is a year of exponential \ngrowth you have permanently destroyed. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1695200,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "psychological trap is assuming your high income \nwill last forever. But corporate restructuring,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1700560,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "burnout, and health issues do not care about your \nmortgage payment. Assuming you will earn top tier  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1706080,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "money indefinitely is a very dangerous game. \nThe specific protocol to fix this is to entirely  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1713280,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reverse the mathematical order of operations in \nyour life. You cannot pay your fixed costs first  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1719440,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and save what is left because there will never \nbe anything left. The methodology is to pay your  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1726000,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "future self first directly from the gross income \nbefore the money ever hits your checking account.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1732960,
    "dDurationMs": 9920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You must force the 15 to 20% savings rate as \nyour absolute primary fixed cost. On $250,000,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1742880,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you automate $50,000 into your 401k backdoor Roth \nand brokerage accounts immediately. Then you pay  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1751280,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your taxes. Then whatever is left over is the hard \nceiling for your housing, cars, and lifestyle.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1759120,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you cannot afford the private school after \npaying yourself first, then you cannot afford the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1764240,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "private school. It is a harsh truth, but it is the \nonly way to break the poverty line trap. You have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1771520,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to artificially constrain your present lifestyle \nto guarantee your future freedom. It takes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1777520,
    "dDurationMs": 8320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "discipline, but the alternative path is incredibly \nrewarding. Let us look at the alternative reality.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1785840,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The road not taken by the typical high earnner. \nImagine the exact same household making  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1792400,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "$250,000 a year. But instead of falling into \nthe prestige trap, they make slightly different  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1798880,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fixed choices. They buy a modest house in a good \nbut not elite public school district. They drive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1806240,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one reliable older car and one modest new car. \nThey utilize public schools instead of private.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1813440,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is not a life of deprivation. They \nare still taking vacations, eating out,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1818400,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and living comfortably. But their structural \nfoundation is built on rock instead of sand.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1824880,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They do not have the quiet desperation humming \nin the background of their lives because they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1829520,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have deliberately chosen a reference group that \nvalues wealth accumulation over wealth display.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1835920,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The common belief is that making this choice \nmakes you cheap or deprivives your children.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1841440,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In reality, it gives your children the greatest \ngift of all. Parents who are present, unstressed,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1847840,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and completely financially secure. Let us look at \nthe math of this alternative reality. Here is the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1855520,
    "dDurationMs": 10880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hard evidence of the alternative path. Same gross \nincome of 250,000, same taxes of roughly 80,000,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1866400,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but their fixed costs look very different. \nHousing is 40,000 instead of 54,000. Private  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1874640,
    "dDurationMs": 10800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "school is zero. Cars are maybe 10,000 a year \ninstead of 21,000. Child care is 20,000. Their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1885440,
    "dDurationMs": 10480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "total fixed costs drop from over 140,000 down to \n70,000. Now let us run the master equation again.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1895920,
    "dDurationMs": 11120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "$170,000 net minus $70,000 fixed equals \n$100,000 left over every single year. They  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1907040,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have nearly $10,000 a month in free cash flow. \nIf they choose to save just 50,000 of that,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1914560,
    "dDurationMs": 9200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is 20% of gross, they still have 50,000 left \nfor incredible vacations and guilt-free spending.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1923760,
    "dDurationMs": 10640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Saving 50,000 a year over 25 years at a 7% return \nyields over $3.3 million. Combined with employer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1934400,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matches and home equity, they easily coast into \na $5 million net worth. That is the structural  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1942560,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "difference between the poverty line and being rich \nenough to retire. It is the exact same salary,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1949600,
    "dDurationMs": 8720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but entirely different fixed choices. The verdict \nhere is clear. Your income does not determine your  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1958320,
    "dDurationMs": 9360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wealth. Your fixed cost ratio determines your \nwealth. A $250,000 salary is an incredible tool.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1967680,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it is just a tool. If you hand that tool over \nto real estate agents, car dealerships, and elite  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1974560,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "private institutions, you will work your entire \nlife to build their wealth instead of your own.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1981680,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The poverty line for high earners is a \nself-imposed prison constructed from social  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1986640,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expectations and ego. But the beautiful thing \nabout a self-imposed prison is that you hold the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1993600,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "key. You can choose to downsize. You can choose \nto drive an older car. You can choose to ignore  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2001280,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the neighborhood arms race. When you do that, you \nreclaim your income and you buy back your future.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2009120,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Stop acting rich today so you can actually be \nwealthy tomorrow. The math never lies, but it is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2016480,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up to you to decide which side of the equation \nyou want to live on. I want to hear your peer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2022800,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "review on this data. Have you felt the pressure \nof the $250,000 poverty line in your own life?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2030400,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Drop a comment below and let me know which fixed \ncost trap is the hardest to avoid in your city.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2036240,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Your real world experiences help ground these \nnumbers, and I read every single one. If this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2042400,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "conversation brought you value, hit the like \nbutton so the algorithm knows to share it with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2046720,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other high earners who might be feeling the exact \nsame silent financial stress. And do not forget to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2052880,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "subscribe so you never miss a breakdown. Thank \nyou for spending your time with me today. Keep  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2058720,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your fixed costs low, guard your free cash \nflow, and I will see you in the next one."
    } ]
  } ]
